※※ What every program starts with, and the devices it starts with open.
※
※ One module for now, named `std` and found beside the compiler.  What a program
※ names here is a *descriptor* -- something that may be read, or written, or
※ both -- and never the machinery underneath it.
※
※ The machinery is the runtime the compiler carries: C, compiled ahead of time
※ for every architecture, reached through `@[external]` and handed the ring as a
※ record laid out the way it reads one.  That there is a ring, and that there is
※ sometimes not one, is not something a program can see.

※※ A device that can be read.
@[export]
type Reader = fd : i32

※※ A device that can be written.
@[export]
type Writer = fd : i32

※※ A device that can be both, which is what a socket and a file opened either
※※ way are.
@[export]
type ReadWriter = fd : i32

※※ The devices a program starts with open.
@[export]
type Io = input : Reader ; output : Writer ; errors : Writer

※※ What a program is started with: the devices it inherited and the words it
※※ was named with.
※
※ A record rather than the two of them outright, so that whatever else a program
※ inherits has somewhere to go without changing the one signature every program
※ writes.  The environment is not one of those: it is a table and not a run of
※ words, so it is `⎕environ` and the compiler builds it.
※
※ `args` is what the kernel put on the stack, counted: the first is the name the
※ program was run under, as it is everywhere, and the rest are what followed it.
※ Each is a `str`, which is bytes and a length -- the kernel's own shape is a
※ nul at the end, and the length is counted once before the program starts
※ rather than by everything that reads one.
@[export]
type Init = io : Io ; args : str⟦⟧

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused.
※
※ The numbers are the kernel's own, so a reader who knows what `EAGAIN` means
※ knows what `would_block` is; the names are the language's, so a reader who
※ does not need not learn them.  `other` is what anything this list does not
※ name comes to -- honest about what it cannot say rather than pretending the
※ list is closed.
※
※ What is named is what a read or a write of a descriptor the program was
※ started with can actually give.  There is no way to open anything, so the
※ refusals that belong to opening are not here: a name nothing can reach would
※ be a comparison every program pays for and none of them needs.
@[export]
enum Error:
    other = 0 ;
    interrupted = 4 ;        ※ EINTR
    device = 5 ;             ※ EIO
    not_a_device = 9 ;       ※ EBADF
    would_block = 11 ;       ※ EAGAIN
    no_memory = 12 ;         ※ ENOMEM
    not_allowed = 13 ;       ※ EACCES
    bad_address = 14 ;       ※ EFAULT
    is_a_directory = 21 ;    ※ EISDIR
    invalid = 22 ;           ※ EINVAL
    too_big = 27 ;           ※ EFBIG
    no_room = 28 ;           ※ ENOSPC
    broken_pipe = 32 ;       ※ EPIPE
    reset = 104              ※ ECONNRESET

※※ What the kernel answered, read as one of those.
※
※ The enumeration's numbers are the kernel's, so this is the question "is this
※ number one of them" and nothing else -- which is what narrowing to an
※ enumeration asks.  Anything it does not name is `other`.
※
※ What `read`, `write` and `flush` carry is the number itself, which cannot be
※ wrong; this is here for a program that would rather compare against a name.
@[export]
fn as_error(n: i32) → Error:
    ⎕narrow(n, ⌜Error⌝) ?? Error.other

※ -- the ring ---------------------------------------------------------------

※※ What the runtime keeps about the ring, laid out the way it reads it.
※
※ This declaration and the one in `runtime/io.c` are written in two languages
※ and nothing makes them one thing, so the C says how big the record is, how it
※ is aligned and where each field lies and how wide, and a test compares.
※
※ Nothing here reads any of it: what this module does with it is hand over a
※ reference, which is what an `@[abi]` record does and all it does.
@[abi]
type Ring =
    state : u64 ; fd : u64 ;
    sq_head : u64 ; sq_tail : u64 ; sq_mask : u64 ; sq_array : u64 ;
    sqes : u64 ;
    cq_head : u64 ; cq_tail : u64 ; cq_mask : u64 ; cqes : u64 ;
    held : u64⟦8⟧ ; answer : i64⟦8⟧

※※ The one ring, made by the runtime the first time anything reads or writes.
let ring: mut Ring = Ring(
    .state ← 0u64, .fd ← 0u64,
    .sq_head ← 0u64, .sq_tail ← 0u64, .sq_mask ← 0u64, .sq_array ← 0u64,
    .sqes ← 0u64,
    .cq_head ← 0u64, .cq_tail ← 0u64, .cq_mask ← 0u64, .cqes ← 0u64,
    .held ← ⟦0u64, 0u64, 0u64, 0u64, 0u64, 0u64, 0u64, 0u64⟧, .answer ← ⟦0i64, 0i64, 0i64, 0i64, 0i64, 0i64, 0i64, 0i64⟧)

※※ Which direction a request goes, as the runtime numbers them.
let READING: i32 = 0i32
let WRITING: i32 = 1i32

@[external("pl4g_io_submit")]
fn io_submit(r: &mut Ring, which: i32, fd: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_wait")]
fn io_wait(r: &mut Ring, slot: i64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_drain")]
fn io_drain(r: &mut Ring)

※※ A request that has been started, and may not have been answered yet.
※
※ What it holds is where the runtime is keeping the request, and nothing else:
※ what the kernel said goes there too, so a request the ring is still working on
※ and one that was done on the spot are the same handle read the same way.  A
※ program that never flushes one loses nothing -- the drain before it ends reads
※ every one that is left.
@[export]
type Pending = slot : i64

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused, as it says it: a positive number,
※※ which is the number `⎕errno` would have been.
fn refusal(of: i64) → i32:
    ⎕narrow(0i64 - of, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32

※※ Wait for everything outstanding.  A program does this before it ends, and
※※ the compiler arranges for it; nothing stops a program doing it itself.
@[export, impure]
fn drain():
    io_drain(&mut ring)

※※ And what makes every program do it.  A destructor runs when the startup
※※ function returns, which is what "exits normally" means.
@[destructor, impure]
fn finish():
    drain()

※※ Start writing `what` to `to`, answering the request that was started.
※
※ It does not wait: the kernel is told about the request when something waits
※ for it, which is `flush` or the drain a program does before it ends.  So
※ several writes may be in flight at once, and a program that wants to know one
※ of them happened says so.
@[export, impure]
fn write(to: &mut Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → Pending ? i32:
    let at: u64 = if #what = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&what⟦0⟧)
    let slot: i64 = io_submit(&mut ring, WRITING, to⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#what))
    if slot < 0i64:
        ※ every slot is holding an answer nobody has read: `EAGAIN`, since what
        ※ would make it work is flushing one of them
        ⊥ 11i32
    else:
        Pending(.slot ← slot)

※※ Wait for a request and answer how many bytes went, or what the kernel said.
@[export, impure]
fn flush(of: Pending) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let got: i64 = io_wait(&mut ring, of.slot)
    if got < 0i64:
        ⊥ refusal(got)
    else:
        ⎕unit(⎕narrow(got, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64, ⌜size⌝)

※※ Write `what` to `to` and wait for it, answering how many bytes went.
※
※ `write` and `flush` in one, which is what a program that has nothing else to
※ do while it waits should say.
@[export, impure]
fn write_sync(to: &mut Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    flush(write(to, what)?)

※※ Read from a device into a run of bytes.
※※
※※ \param from the device to read from
※※ \param into where to put what comes, and how much to ask for: as many bytes
※※ as it holds
※※ \return how many bytes came, and nought where there is no more to come
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
※
※ There is no form of this that does not wait: what a read is for is the bytes,
※ and a program that has not waited does not have them.
@[export, impure]
fn read(from: &mut Reader, into: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let at: u64 = if #into = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&into⟦0⟧)
    let slot: i64 = io_submit(&mut ring, READING, from⌖.fd, at, ⎕drop(#into))
    if slot < 0i64:
        ⊥ 11i32
    else:
        flush(Pending(.slot ← slot))

※ -- writing text ------------------------------------------------------------

※※ Several runs of bytes written as one request, which is what `println` is for.
let WRITING_SEVERAL: i32 = 2i32

※※ Write `total` bytes from `at` to `to`, offering what is left until none is.
※
※ The kernel may take fewer bytes than it was offered -- a pipe with room for
※ some of them, a signal part way -- and a program that printed a line wants the
※ line printed.  So this is the loop `write_sync` does not do, and it is here
※ rather than in each caller because both of the two want it.
@[impure]
fn all_of(to: &mut Writer, at: u64, total: u64) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let done: mut u64 = 0u64
    while done < total:
        let slot: i64 = io_submit(&mut ring, WRITING, to⌖.fd, at + done,
                                  total - done)
        if slot < 0i64:
            return ⊥ 11i32
        let went: u64 = ⎕drop(flush(Pending(.slot ← slot))?)
        if went = 0u64:
            ※ No progress and no refusal: nothing more will happen, and saying so
            ※ beats asking for ever.  `EIO` is what the kernel would have said.
            return ⊥ 5i32
        done ← done + went
    ⎕unit(done, ⌜size⌝)

※※ Write every byte of `what` to `to`, answering how many went.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param what the bytes to write
※※ \return how many bytes went, which is all of them
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
@[export, impure]
fn print(to: &mut Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let total: u64 = ⎕drop(#what)
    if total = 0u64:
        ⎕unit(0u64, ⌜size⌝)
    else:
        all_of(to, ⎕address(&what⟦0⟧), total)

※※ The same, with a newline after it, written as one request.
※※
※※ \details One request and not two: `writev` takes a list of runs of bytes, so
※※ the text and the newline reach the kernel together and nothing else writing to
※※ the same device can land between them.  Where the kernel takes fewer bytes than
※※ it was offered the rest is written plainly, which is simpler than walking the
※※ list and is the uncommon case.
※※
※※ \param to the device to write to
※※ \param what the bytes to write before the newline
※※ \return how many bytes went, which is `#what` and one more
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
@[export, impure]
fn println(to: &mut Writer, what: u8⟦⟧) → u64 ¤size ? i32:
    let newline: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕bytes("\n")
    let text: u64 = ⎕drop(#what)
    let after: u64 = ⎕address(&newline⟦0⟧)
    if text = 0u64:
        all_of(to, after, 1u64)
    else:
        let before: u64 = ⎕address(&what⟦0⟧)
        ※ The kernel's `iovec` twice over: where the bytes are and how many.
        let parts: mut u64⟦4⟧ = ⟦before, text, after, 1u64⟧
        let slot: i64 = io_submit(&mut ring, WRITING_SEVERAL, to⌖.fd,
                                 ⎕address(&parts⟦0⟧), 2u64)
        if slot < 0i64:
            return ⊥ 11i32
        let went: u64 = ⎕drop(flush(Pending(.slot ← slot))?)
        let total: u64 = text + 1u64
        if went = total:
            ⎕unit(total, ⌜size⌝)
        elif went < text:
            ⎕drop(all_of(to, before + went, text - went)?)
            all_of(to, after, 1u64)
        else:
            all_of(to, after + (went - text), total - went)

※ -- describing a build --------------------------------------------------------

※※ What a build function is handed, and what it writes into to say what to build.
※※
※※ \details A function marked `@[build]` is run by the compiler rather than
※※ compiled into anything, and what it leaves in this object is what gets built.
※※ The fields are the settings a command line would otherwise carry; the run of
※※ things to build is added to with `add_executable`, there being no way to write
※※ a growing list of them in the language yet.
※※
※※ \note A field left alone keeps what the command line said, so a build file
※※ says only what it means to decide.
@[export]
type Build =
    ※ Where what is built is written.  A relative name is relative to where the
    ※ compiler was run, as every other name on a command line is.
    output_dir : str ;
    ※ What to build for, written the way `--target` writes it.
    target : str ;
    ※ How hard to work on the code, which is what `-O` says.
    opt_level : u8 ;
    ※ Which of the target's microarchitecture levels may be used, or nothing
    ※ where the target's default is wanted.
    mclevel : str ;
    ※ How much room a program's own stack has, and how much unreachable space
    ※ sits below it.
    stack_size : u64 ;
    guard_size : u64 ;
    ※ What the compiler was run with, read the way a program reads its own.
    ※ Nothing may be put in it: a build reads the environment and does not
    ※ make one.
    env : ⸨str: str⸩

※※ Add a program to what this build builds.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what the program is called, which is also what the file is called
※※ \param sources the files it is built from, of which there must be at least one
@[export, builtin]
fn add_executable(b: &mut Build, name: str, sources: str⟦⟧)

※※ What the command line said about a name, or what the build file chose.
※※
※※ \details `-Dname=value` on the command line is how a build is told something
※※ from outside; this is how the build function reads it.  A name the command
※※ line said nothing about answers with the fallback, so a build file always has
※※ an answer and never has to ask whether there was one.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what to look for, which is what `-D` writes before the `=`
※※ \param fallback what to answer with where the command line said nothing
※※ \return what was said, or the fallback
@[export, builtin]
fn option(b: &mut Build, name: str, fallback: str) → str

※※ The same question, asked of something that is either so or not.
※※
※※ \details `-Dname` on its own means it is so; `-Dname=` takes `true`, `yes`,
※※ `on` and `1` for so and anything else for not.
※※
※※ \param b what the build is described in
※※ \param name what to look for
※※ \param fallback what to answer with where the command line said nothing
※※ \return whether it is so
@[export, builtin]
fn option_flag(b: &mut Build, name: str, fallback: bool) → bool
